> The shorter compositions (of two or three nouns), OTOH, are used
> in many languages as far as I see.

Of course all languages can express the same concept. How they do it
and how expressive it is depends on the language and the concept.

Noun compounds are of course a grammatical construct that is available
in many languages. But it is also true that German has taken
compounding noun to a different level than most other languages.

Because German and English both are Germanic languages English has
also a reasonable amount of noun compound although not as many as
German. In contrast Romance languages usually don't use noun
compounds.

